up to $10,000 locked for at least one year and want a permanent 1.40% fixed rate on top of inflation protection. They are not worth it if you need the funds inside 12 months or if a savings account provides a higher return after accounting for the three-month penalty."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How are I bonds taxed?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Interest earned on I bonds is subject to federal income tax, but it is completely exempt from state and local income taxes. You can report the interest annually or defer reporting until you redeem the bonds, and qualified higher education expenses may allow you to exclude the interest from federal tax using IRS Form 8815."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I lose money in an I bond?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, you cannot lose principal in an I bond. TreasuryDirect rules set a floor at zero percent for the combined composite rate, meaning deflation will never reduce your bond value. However, cashing out before five years forfeits the last three months of interest earnings."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"When can I cash in an I bond?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You can cash in a Series I bond after holding it for 12 months. If you redeem between 12 months and five years, you forfeit the last three months of interest. After five full years of ownership, you can redeem at any time with no penalty."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is this financial advice?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. This page provides general information based on TreasuryDirect pages retrieved on November 18, 2026, and does not constitute financial or tax advice. Interest rates for new Series I savings bonds reset twice a year, on May 1 and November 1."}}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/","jobTitle":"Personal Finance Writer & Editor","description":"Jonathan Velez writes and edits ModernWallet's personal-finance content, focused on loans, interest, and debt payoff. Every calculator page is reviewed against primary sources like the CFPB and Federal Reserve.","knowsAbout":["Auto loans","Loan amortization","Interest and APR","Debt payoff strategies","Personal finance"],"worksFor":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com"}}</script> <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Are I Bonds a Good Investment in November 2026?","description":"Are I bonds a good investment right now? Compare the 5.51% composite rate, rules, 12-month lock, tax benefits, and alternatives before you buy.","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/guides/are-i-bonds-a-good-investment/","image":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"},"datePublished":"2026-11-18","dateModified":"2026-11-18","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/"},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"}},"citation":[{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"TreasuryDirect I bonds","url":"https://www.treasurydirect.gov/savings-bonds/i-bonds/"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"TreasuryDirect I bonds interest rates","url":"https://www.treasurydirect.gov/savings-bonds/i-bonds/i-bonds-interest-rates/"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"TreasuryDirect buying savings bonds","url":"https://www.treasurydirect.gov/savings-bonds/buy-a-bond/"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"TreasuryDirect tax information for EE and I bonds","url":"https://www.treasurydirect.gov/savings-bonds/tax-information-ee-i-bonds/"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"TreasuryDirect using bonds for higher education","url":"https://www.treasurydirect.gov/savings-bonds/tax-information-ee-i-bonds/using-bonds-for-higher-education/"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"TreasuryDirect comparison of TIPS and I bonds","url":"https://www.treasurydirect.gov/research-center/history-of-savings-bond/comparing-tips-to-i/"}]}</script> <main class="page" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <a href="/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>ModernWallet</a> <span data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>/</span> <a href="/guides/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Guides</a> <span data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>/</span> <span class="crumb-current" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Are I Bonds a Good Investment in November 2026?</span> </nav> <header class="hero" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h1 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Are I Bonds a Good Investment in November 2026?</h1> <div class="byline" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr> <span class="by" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>By <a href="/about/" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Jonathan Velez</a>, Personal Finance Writer &amp; Editor</span> <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>·</span> <span class="rev" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Reviewed against primary sources · Updated November 18, 2026</span> </div>   <p class="lede" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>I bonds are a good investment for cash you will not need for at least 12 months and want protected from inflation, up to $10,000 per person per calendar year. As of November 18, 2026, the Series I savings bond composite rate is 5.51% for bonds issued November 1, 2026 to April 30, 2027.</p><p class="lede" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>That figure includes a 1.40% fixed rate that never changes for the life of the bond. If you need your cash inside a year or hold a savings account yielding more than the effective return after the early redemption penalty, a savings account is the better choice.</p> </header> <section class="tools" aria-label="Tools for this journey" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <span class="tools-title" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Tools for this journey</span> <div class="tool-pills" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <a class="tool-pill" href="/inflation-calculator/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Inflation<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a><a class="tool-pill" href="/hysa-vs-cd-calculator/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>High-yield savings vs CD<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a> </div> </section>   <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Who Should Buy I Bonds Right Now</h2> <p>I bonds fit buyers who want to protect up to $10,000 against inflation and will not touch the principal for at least one full year.</p><p>According to the <a href="https://www.treasurydirect.gov/savings-bonds/i-bonds/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TreasuryDirect I bonds page</a>, Series I savings bonds issued from November 1, 2026 to April 30, 2027 pay a composite rate of 5.51%. That composite consists of two parts: a 1.40% fixed rate and a semiannual inflation rate of 2.04% announced on November 1, 2026. The 1.40% fixed rate never changes for as long as you own the bond. The inflation rate resets every six months based on inflation measurements, which means your combined earnings adjust twice a year. If deflation occurs, the combined rate stops at zero, so your rate never drops below zero percent.</p><p>Your timeline and cash needs decide whether an I bond beats the alternatives. A high-yield savings account works better if you need liquidity before 12 months pass.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="prose-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Reader Situation</th><th scope="col">Recommendation</th><th scope="col">Primary Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Money needed inside 12 months</th><td>Skip I bonds</td><td>TreasuryDirect rules strictly prohibit cashing in before 12 months</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Money set aside for 1 to 5 years</th><td>Buy I bonds</td><td>The 1.40% fixed rate stays permanent, though you forfeit the last 3 months of interest if redeemed before 5 years</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Cash above $10,000 per person</th><td>Split between options</td><td>Purchases are capped at $10,000 in electronic bonds per Social Security number per calendar year</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Investor seeking a permanent real return</th><td>Buy I bonds</td><td>The 1.40% fixed component remains attached to the bond for up to 30 years</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For investors who want an inflation-protected asset, marketable securities like Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities offer an alternative route. See our <a href="/compare/i-bonds-vs-tips/">I bonds vs TIPS</a> guide for how those instruments differ in liquidity and limits. If you want a fixed-rate government bond without inflation adjustments, compare choices in our <a href="/compare/i-bonds-vs-ee-bonds/">I bonds vs EE bonds</a> guide.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>I Bond Rules That Decide the Answer</h2> <p>Five operational rules established by the United States Department of the Treasury determine whether Series I bonds work for your portfolio.</p><p>You face an absolute 12-month lock. Under Treasury rules, you cannot cash in an I bond during the first 12 months after purchase. If an emergency occurs at month eight, your money remains completely unavailable in your account.</p><p>Cashing out before five years triggers an interest penalty. If you redeem a bond before holding it for five years, you lose the last three months of interest. For example, if you cash in a bond at 18 months, TreasuryDirect pays you the first 15 months of interest and keeps the final three months. Values displayed for bonds under five years old inside your TreasuryDirect account already exclude those last three months of interest. Once you reach five years of ownership, the penalty disappears entirely.</p><p>Annual purchases have a strict ceiling. You can buy up to $10,000 in electronic I bonds per calendar year per Social Security number. The minimum electronic purchase is $25, and you can buy in any amount to the penny above that threshold.</p><p>Purchases are electronic only. Since January 1, 2025, purchases are electronic only through a TreasuryDirect account per the <a href="https://www.treasurydirect.gov/savings-bonds/buy-a-bond/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TreasuryDirect buying savings bonds page</a>. For other savings bonds, the Series EE bond pays a 2.90% rate for bonds issued November 1, 2026 to April 30, 2027.</p><p>Interest pays out at redemption. I bonds earn interest until you cash them in or until they reach their 30-year maturity date. You receive no regular cash distributions into a bank account along the way. All accumulated interest is paid when you redeem the bond or when the bond matures.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>I Bonds vs a High-Yield Savings Account Over 12 Months</h2> <p>Headline yields do not tell the whole story because the three-month interest penalty reduces your real payout at month 12.</p><p>In our internal modeling, we compared $10,000 in an I bond with $10,000 in a savings account over 12 months using Python calculations based on published rate formulas. Assuming an initial investment of $10,000 bought in November 2026, the bond earns the published 5.51% composite rate for the first six months, bringing the month-six principal to $10,275.50. The second-six-month composite rate resets on May 1, 2027, and its future value is an unknown variable. Because redeeming at month 12 causes the loss of the last three months of interest, you only collect nine months of total interest.</p><p>The table below shows the calculated outcomes under three assumed second-half composite rates (1.0%, 3.0%, and 5.51% annualized) alongside savings accounts with assumed yields of 3.5%, 4.0%, and 4.5% Annual Percentage Yield (APY). Savings account yields and second-half I bond rates are labeled assumptions, not quotes or forecasts. Results are pre-tax and use simplified accrual, whereas TreasuryDirect account records are authoritative.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="prose-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Investment Option</th><th scope="col">Assumed Rate Details</th><th scope="col">Ending Balance at Month 12</th><th scope="col">12-Month Dollar Gain</th><th scope="col">Effective 12-Month Return</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">I Bond (Assumed 1.0% second half)</th><td>5.51% for 6 mos, 1.0% for 6 mos, minus 3 mos penalty</td><td>$10,301.19</td><td>$301.19</td><td>3.01%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">I Bond (Assumed 3.0% second half)</th><td>5.51% for 6 mos, 3.0% for 6 mos, minus 3 mos penalty</td><td>$10,352.57</td><td>$352.57</td><td>3.53%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">I Bond (Assumed 5.51% second half)</th><td>5.51% for 12 mos, minus 3 mos penalty</td><td>$10,417.05</td><td>$417.05</td><td>4.17%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Savings Account (Assumed 3.5% APY)</th><td>Constant 3.5% APY with full liquidity</td><td>$10,350.00</td><td>$350.00</td><td>3.50%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Savings Account (Assumed 4.0% APY)</th><td>Constant 4.0% APY with full liquidity</td><td>$10,400.00</td><td>$400.00</td><td>4.00%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Savings Account (Assumed 4.5% APY)</th><td>Constant 4.5% APY with full liquidity</td><td>$10,450.00</td><td>$450.00</td><td>4.50%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Our modeling illustrates how the early redemption penalty drags down the headline 5.51% rate over a single year. If the second-half composite rate drops to 1.0%, an investor redeeming at month 12 earns an effective 3.01% return ($301.19), trailing all three assumed savings yields. Even if the composite rate stays at 5.51% through the May 1, 2027 reset, the three-month penalty caps the 12-month gain at $417.05, representing an effective return of 4.17%.</p><p>Consider the counterexample revealed by the math. If the I bond composite remains at 5.51% and a competing savings account yields 3.5% APY, the I bond wins at month 12 by delivering $417.05 versus $350.00. However, if a savings account yields 4.5% APY, the savings account produces $450.00 and wins against all three I bond scenarios. At an assumed 4.0% savings yield, the savings account beats the I bond unless the bond composite rate stays near 5.51%.</p><p>If you hold the I bond for 15 months instead of 12, you collect all 12 months of interest while sacrificing only the three extra months. In our modeling, redeeming at month 15 yields ending values of $10,326.88 under the 1.0% second-half assumption, $10,429.63 under the 3.0% assumption, and $10,558.59 if the composite stays at 5.51%. Read more about rate resets on our <a href="/guides/i-bond-interest-rate/">I bond interest rate</a> page, or test broader inflation assumptions using our <a href="/inflation-calculator/">inflation calculator</a>.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Taxes and the Education Exclusion</h2> <p>Series I savings bonds follow specific federal and state tax rules.</p><p>According to the <a href="https://www.treasurydirect.gov/savings-bonds/tax-information-ee-i-bonds/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TreasuryDirect tax information page</a>, earnings on I bonds are subject to federal income tax, but they are completely exempt from state and local income taxes. They are also subject to federal estate, gift, and excise taxes, as well as state estate or inheritance taxes.</p><p>TreasuryDirect allows you to choose between two federal reporting methods. You can report interest every year as it accrues, or you can defer reporting the interest until you cash in the bond or reach the 30-year maturity date. Most bond owners choose to defer reporting until redemption. When you eventually redeem the bond and receive the cash, TreasuryDirect issues a Form 1099-INT detailing the taxable interest.</p><p>You may also qualify to exclude all or part of the interest from federal income tax under the education tax exclusion. Details outlined on the <a href="https://www.treasurydirect.gov/savings-bonds/tax-information-ee-i-bonds/using-bonds-for-higher-education/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TreasuryDirect higher education page</a> specify several strict qualification requirements:</p><ul class="prose-list"><li>The bonds must be Series EE or Series I savings bonds issued after 1989.</li><li>The bonds must be registered in your name as the owner. If a child is listed as the owner, the exclusion cannot be claimed, so parents must retain ownership.</li><li>You must have been 24 years of age or older before the issue date of the bonds.</li><li>Your modified adjusted gross income must sit below the annual threshold limits established by the Internal Revenue Service.</li><li>You must cash the bonds during the exact same tax year in which you pay qualified higher education expenses at an eligible educational institution for yourself, your spouse, or a dependent.</li><li>Your tax filing status cannot be married filing separately.</li></ul><p>To claim the education exclusion, you file IRS Form 8815 alongside your federal tax return when reporting the redemption.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>When I Bonds Are the Wrong Tool</h2> <p>I bonds are the wrong financial instrument in three common situations.</p><p>Do not use I bonds for your core emergency fund. The 12-month lock means you cannot withdraw principal during the first year under any circumstances. If your car breaks down or an unexpected medical bill arrives in month six, those bond funds cannot help you. Before locking dollars away in a TreasuryDirect account, assess your liquid cash buffer by reading our guide on <a href="/guides/how-much-emergency-fund/">how much emergency fund</a> you need in an immediately accessible account.</p><p>Do not rely on I bonds if you need to park large sums of money above the annual ceiling. Because Treasury limits purchases to $10,000 per Social Security number each calendar year, you cannot place a larger balance in I bonds. For larger cash allocations that require stability and predictable yields, evaluate fixed-term bank products through our <a href="/compare/hysa-vs-cd/">HYSA vs CD</a> comparison.</p><p>Do not choose I bonds when marketable government paper or bank deposits deliver higher after-tax yields. The <a href="https://www.treasurydirect.gov/research-center/history-of-savings-bond/comparing-tips-to-i/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TreasuryDirect TIPS comparison page</a> notes that TIPS have a $100 minimum purchase and can be sold before maturity, whereas I bonds are non-marketable. To explore how marketable short-term paper stacks up against bank certificates, review our <a href="/compare/cd-vs-treasury-bill/">CD vs Treasury bill</a> analysis.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Our Takeaway</h2> <p>Buy Series I savings bonds when you have up to $10,000 you can afford to leave untouched for at least 12 months, and you want an inflation-linked return with a 1.40% fixed rate that remains locked for up to 30 years. Skip them entirely for emergency funds, for sums exceeding the $10,000 annual cap, or whenever a liquid savings account yields more than the effective return after the three-month penalty.</p><p>In our internal modeling, we observed that an early redemption at month 12 reduces an initial 5.51% headline yield to an effective return between 3.01% and 4.17%, depending on where the semiannual rate resets on May 1, 2027. This evaluation reflects official TreasuryDirect rules and our calculated models. What would change our answer is the upcoming May 1, 2027 rate reset, your need for immediate cash access, your current savings yield, or changes to your tax situation.</p><p>To decide whether are I bonds a good investment for your goals, check your savings yield against the I bond table, then decide whether you can leave $10,000 untouched for a year.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="faqs" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Are I bonds still earning interest?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Yes. Series I bonds earn interest until you cash them in or until they reach their 30-year maturity date. For bonds issued from November 1, 2026 to April 30, 2027, the initial annual composite rate is 5.51%.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>How much can I buy in I bonds?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>You can purchase up to $10,000 in electronic I bonds per calendar year per Social Security number. The minimum electronic purchase through TreasuryDirect is $25, and you can buy any amount to the penny above that.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Are I bonds worth it right now?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>I bonds are worth buying right now if you can leave up to $10,000 locked for at least one year and want a permanent 1.40% fixed rate on top of inflation protection. They are not worth it if you need the funds inside 12 months or if a savings account provides a higher return after accounting for the three-month penalty.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>How are I bonds taxed?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Interest earned on I bonds is subject to federal income tax, but it is completely exempt from state and local income taxes. You can report the interest annually or defer reporting until you redeem the bonds, and qualified higher education expenses may allow you to exclude the interest from federal tax using IRS Form 8815.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Can I lose money in an I bond?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>No, you cannot lose principal in an I bond. TreasuryDirect rules set a floor at zero percent for the combined composite rate, meaning deflation will never reduce your bond value. However, cashing out before five years forfeits the last three months of interest earnings.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>When can I cash in an I bond?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>You can cash in a Series I bond after holding it for 12 months. If you redeem between 12 months and five years, you forfeit the last three months of interest. After five full years of ownership, you can redeem at any time with no penalty.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Is this financial advice?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>No. This page provides general information based on TreasuryDirect pages retrieved on November 18, 2026, and does not constitute financial or tax advice. Interest rates for new Series I savings bonds reset twice a year, on May 1 and November 1.</p> </details> </div> </section>  <section class="section sources" data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x><h2 data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x>Sources</h2><p class="source-note" data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x>
